#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"grouping-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Group operator terms: use the operator structure {#group-operator-terms-use-"
"the-operator-structure}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A [`BosonOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator\") can tag each of its terms with a "
"**group index**. The tags carry no meaning of their own: they only say which"
" terms belong together. What the tags *mean* is decided by whatever consumes"
" them. In this package the consumers are the time-evolution gate, which is "
"split into one factor per group, and the randomized qDRIFT pass, which "
"samples whole groups.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide shows how to assign groups, how to check the properties a "
"consumer relies on, and how to lay the operator out so that picking a few "
"groups out of a large one stays cheap.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  No automatic grouping strategy ships with this package. You assign the indices yourself, from what you know about your model.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Assign groups {#assign-groups}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The running example is a three-site Bose-Hubbard chain. Each bond "
"contributes a hopping pair, and the on-site interactions form a diagonal "
"piece. The simplest way to build a grouped operator is "
"[`from_terms_with_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_terms_with_groups "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_terms_with_groups\"), which "
"takes each term together with its group index as an `(actions, coefficient, "
"group)` triple. You decide the group at the moment you write the term down, "
"when you still know why it belongs there:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The terms are stored in the order you give them, so the group indices cannot"
" end up attached to the wrong terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An operator that already exists has to be grouped after the fact, by "
"assigning the [`groups`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups"
" \"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.groups\") attribute. That is where "
"care is needed, because "
"[`from_dict()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.from_dict\") does not specify the "
"order in which it stores its terms. Writing the list out by position can "
"then attach an index to the wrong term. Derive each index from the term "
"itself instead:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The number of indices has to match the number of terms. Anything else is "
"rejected, because a too-short list would silently drop trailing terms "
"wherever terms are iterated together with their groups:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check what the consumer relies on {#check-what-the-consumer-relies-on}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Three functions in "
"[`qiskit_bosons.operators.terms`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms#module-qiskit_bosons.operators.terms "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms\") answer one narrow question each about an "
"existing grouping. None of them says a grouping is “correct”, because "
"correctness is relative to the consumer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Is each group Hermitian?** This is the property that matters most. The "
"time evolution of a group is only unitary if the group is Hermitian, and a "
"lone hopping term $b^\\dagger_i b_j$ is *not*. Only the pair with its "
"adjoint is Hermitian:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A grouping that splits a hopping pair is caught immediately:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The four hopping terms are caught. The three `True` entries are the on-site "
"terms $b^\\dagger_i b^\\dagger_i b_i b_i$, which are Hermitian by "
"themselves. A mode can legitimately repeat within a term, and nothing is "
"dropped for doing so. The flags follow the order of `terms`, which is why "
"each one can be read against the term it describes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Are the coefficients uniform?** A randomized product formula weighs a "
"group by the mean *magnitude* of its coefficients, which is a faithful "
"description of the group only if the magnitudes agree:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Neither check implies the other: uniform coefficients do not make a group "
"Hermitian, and a Hermitian group can mix magnitudes. By default the "
"magnitudes are compared, because that is what "
"[`group_coeff_means()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-terms-group-coeff-"
"means#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_coeff_means "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_coeff_means\") averages. A Hermitian "
"pair with complex coefficients has equal magnitudes but unequal "
"coefficients, so it satisfies only the default form:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  [`groups_are_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-terms-groups-are-hermitian#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian \"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian\") is built on [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-boson-operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.is_hermitian \"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.is_hermitian\"), which compares an operator with its adjoint after normal ordering. That is an identity of the untruncated algebra, so a group that passes is Hermitian as a bosonic operator. See [Design principles of operator representations](operators#operators-explanation) for how products of ladder operators are ordered and compared.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Order the terms by group {#order-the-terms-by-group}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[`split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"bosons/operators-boson-"
"operator#qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.split_out_groups "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.BosonOperator.split_out_groups\") returns one "
"operator per requested group. When the terms of a group are scattered "
"through the operator, it has to scan every term to find them. When each "
"group forms one contiguous run, it binary-searches the group boundaries "
"instead, so the cost of a lookup follows the number of groups *requested* "
"rather than the number of terms *held*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[`group_order()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-bosons/operators-"
"terms-group-order#qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_order "
"\"qiskit_bosons.operators.terms.group_order\") produces that layout. It is a"
" stable sort by group index: the value of the operator does not change, and "
"terms within a group keep their relative order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Both layouts return the same operators; only the cost differs:\n"
msgstr ""

